course: dictionary
lesson: 15966
title: Untruthfulness
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Untruthfulness is a noun. It means the habit of not telling the truth, which is being dishonest or lying. You can work out its meaning from its parts: un- means not, truthful means honest, and -ness turns the word into a noun for a quality.
explain: «Mia's untruthfulness made her friends stop trusting her.» Mia kept saying things that were not true. The clue is that her friends stopped trusting her. Lying breaks trust, so untruthfulness must mean lying or being dishonest.
explain: In stories, untruthfulness has consequences. In «The Boy Who Cried Wolf», the boy tells lies for fun, so when a real wolf comes, nobody believes him. In church talk, the Bible tells people not to bear false witness, which means not to tell lies about others. Truthfulness is a value; untruthfulness breaks trust.
fact: Untruthfulness means being dishonest, or lying.
fact: Untruthfulness can also mean the falseness of a statement, which is when something said is not true.
fact: Truthfulness is the opposite: it means being honest and telling the truth.
fact: The word is built from un- (not), truthful (honest) and -ness (a quality).
fact: In «The Boy Who Cried Wolf», untruthfulness leads to nobody believing the boy.
exercise: «Her untruthfulness made her friends stop trusting her.» What does untruthfulness mean here? | Being late for school | *Being dishonest or lying | Running very fast | Being very kind to everyone
exercise: Which word is the opposite of untruthfulness? | Carelessness | *Truthfulness | Loudness | Happiness
exercise: Which part of untruthfulness means "not"? | -ness | *un- | truth | -ful
test: In «The Boy Who Cried Wolf», the boy's untruthfulness leads to what? | He becomes a king | *Nobody believes him, even when he tells the truth | Everyone believes him every time | The wolf goes away
test: A statement that is not true shows what? | Patience | Kindness | Truthfulness | *Untruthfulness
exam: Which sentence shows untruthfulness? | *Sam said he had finished his homework, but he had not even started it | Sam shared his lunch with a friend | Sam said he was tired and went to bed early | Sam helped his sister carry the bags
